# https://www.boostcamp.app/blogs/toji-fushiguro-workout-routine
## **Toji Fushiguro Overview**
Yes, Toji Fushiguro is an anime character, but he is absolutely jacked, [**shredded**](https://www.boostcamp.app/blogs/how-to-properly-cut-weight), and powerful. It is said that he stands at 5’9”-6’2”. He is an immensely popular character from the anime/manga Jujutsu Kaisen, and is known very well not only for his lightning-fast speed as an athletic assassin but also for his aesthetically impressive and muscular physique. Understandably, many fans aspire to both look and perform like Toji. This is the inspiration behind the creation of a 4-day-a-week [**athletic bodybuilding**](https://www.boostcamp.app/blogs/bodybuilding-workouts-for-athletes) plan designed to help you achieve just that.

While he is fictional, that does not mean we cannot workout like he would, in order to obtain a physique like his. That being said, in order to get massive, strong, and be functional, what exactly would you do?

### **Rest Time Guidelines**
Rest periods between lifts are absolutely essential to help you be prepared for the next set, so you can maximize what you are doing. For major [**compound**](https://www.boostcamp.app/blogs/compound-vs-isolation-exercises) exercises such as the barbell [**overhead press**](https://www.boostcamp.app/blogs/mastering-overhead-press-a-beginners-guide), back [**squats**](https://www.boostcamp.app/blogs/how-to-improve-your-barbell-squat), and [**power cleans**](https://www.boostcamp.app/blogs/mastering-the-power-clean-exercise), it's recommended to rest for approximately 2-4 minutes between sets.

Supersets are also utilized in this program, which is where you will be performing two exercises back to back with no rest period. When it comes to the muscle-building and athletic performance supersets, allow yourself about a minute of rest between movements and rest for 1-3 minutes between each round of the supersets.

### **What Exercises are in the Toji Fushiguro Workout?**
Toji Fushiguro’s workout includes a mix of exercises, from your standard compound and isolation exercises to functional movements that help with speed and explosive power. The Toji Fushiguro workouts include exercises such as:

- Squats

- [**Pull-ups**](https://www.boostcamp.app/blogs/pull-ups-vs-chin-ups-what-is-the-best-upper-body-builder)

- Jump variations

- [**Romanian deadlifts**](https://www.boostcamp.app/blogs/romanian-deadlift-vs-deadlift)

- [**Bicep**](https://www.boostcamp.app/blogs/best-short-head-bicep-exercises) curls

- Power cleans


The Toji Fushiguro workout includes a mix of barbell and [**dumbbell**](https://www.boostcamp.app/blogs/dumbbell-training-guide) movements, as well as [**bodyweight exercises**](https://www.boostcamp.app/blogs/no-equipment-workout-get-fit-at-home). You are getting a true all around workout program that is helping you to build strength, size, and power. This differs from most workout programs that have you focusing on one of these aspects, rather than all of them at once.

### **Training Intensity Guidelines**
[**Intensity**](https://www.boostcamp.app/blogs/high-intensity-vs-high-volume-training) is how hard you are going during the workouts, and the Toji Fushiguro workout is certainly an intense one. This program incorporates the standard RPE ( [**Rating of Perceived Exertion**](https://www.boostcamp.app/blogs/ultimate-guide-to-rpe-in-lifting)) system to guide your intensity level during each set.

As far as the intensity and volume behind this workout, you will typically be around an RPE of 7 or 8, so it is definitely not for the faint of heart. Although the RPE is relatively high, you still are doing high volume, going from sets of 3 to 30 reps each workout, depending on the exercise that you are doing of course. For example, on power cleans you may doing sets of 3, but then on pogo jumps you may be doing 30 reps.

When applying this system to explosive plyometric movements, such as kettlebell swings or power cleans, focus on the speed and explosiveness of the movement rather than counting reps until [**failure**](https://www.boostcamp.app/blogs/training-to-failure). For instance, if you're aiming for an RPE of 7 during a set of kettlebell swings, choose a weight that allows you to finish the set with about 3 reps left before you notice a significant decrease in speed and explosiveness in your movements. This approach ensures you're working at an appropriate intensity level to maximize the effectiveness of the exercise.

### **Warm Ups Are Essential**
For a workout routine like this, you cannot begin the workout without a proper [**warm up**](https://www.boostcamp.app/blogs/importance-of-warm-ups-before-weightlifting). A proper warm up will help get the blood flowing and get you loosened up so your body is prepared to lift. The warm up exercises that are included in the Toji Fushiguro routine are as follows:

- 5 Minutes [**Moderate Intensity Cardio**](https://www.boostcamp.app/blogs/how-important-is-cardio)

- World’s Greatest Stretch x12/side

- Hamstring Sweeps x20

- Arm circles x30

## **Who is the Toshi Fushiguro Workout for?**
You do not have to be an anime fan to enjoy the Toshi Fushiguro workout. This program is designed for any intermediate to advanced lifter, and can be done with basic garage gym equipment. If you are looking to become more powerful, ripped, and athletic, then this program may be the one for you.

## **Wrap Up**
Overall, Toji Fushiguro’s workout is definitely an intense routine that is not for those who just want to go through the motions. This workout routine is aimed at building strength, size, and power. That being said, are you going to give this routine a try?

